WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:19.698
Hello. My name is Mohammad Ali Jaffry. I am an architect. With ten years of architectural experience. Welcome, to this episode. The map of the series.

2
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:34.695
Seventeen answers.

3
00:00:35.735 --> 00:00:37.581
One question.

4
00:00:38.621 --> 00:00:45.250
This series is about how artificial intelligence has changed the way we build microservices — and about the discipline of proving it.

5
00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:48.192
The question is precise.

6
00:00:49.232 --> 00:00:52.065
Did AI change microservice architecture?

7
00:00:53.105 --> 00:00:59.200
And under that, five headings: design, boundaries, communication, operations, and the tradeoffs that come with all of it.

8
00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:03.430
Every episode in this series serves one of those five.

9
00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:08.799
The research was written as answers, in the order a researcher needed them.

10
00:01:09.179 --> 00:01:14.055
Read for the argument, they fall into a different order, and that order is the series.

11
00:01:14.435 --> 00:01:17.105
First the question and its terms.

12
00:01:18.145 --> 00:01:25.459
Then the baseline: the history of the style and its pattern language, with every pattern shown in real software.

13
00:01:26.499 --> 00:01:29.030
Then the inside of one service.

14
00:01:30.070 --> 00:01:33.135
Then the platform underneath and the product on top.

15
00:01:33.835 --> 00:01:35.925
Then the yardstick we grade with.

16
00:01:36.965 --> 00:01:39.612
Then the AI system and its own patterns.

17
00:01:40.652 --> 00:01:47.537
And last, what actually changed: the call to the model acquired an architecture, and the business boundary did not.

18
00:01:48.857 --> 00:01:51.086
One rule runs through everything.

19
00:01:52.126 --> 00:01:56.689
A claim that AI changed the architecture has to name the decision it changed.

20
00:01:57.069 --> 00:01:59.298
Not ‘we added an agent’.

21
00:02:00.338 --> 00:02:02.044
Which boundary moved.

22
00:02:03.084 --> 00:02:04.919
Which pipe got smarter.

23
00:02:05.959 --> 00:02:08.687
Which pattern moved from code into a sidecar.

24
00:02:09.727 --> 00:02:14.685
Every chapter ends by fixing one of these rules, and the map collects them all in one table.

25
00:02:15.665 --> 00:02:18.695
The map also carries the reference tools.

26
00:02:19.735 --> 00:02:26.190
A concordance that lines the nine characteristics of the 2014 article up against the chapters.

27
00:02:26.570 --> 00:02:32.224
A master timeline from 1968 to 2026.

28
00:02:33.264 --> 00:02:38.976
The cases the series argues from — Amazon, Netflix, Uber, Airbnb, Shopify, Stripe.

29
00:02:39.356 --> 00:02:41.411
And a glossary of every term.

30
00:02:42.451 --> 00:02:45.911
When a chapter points at another, the map is where you look it up.

31
00:02:46.891 --> 00:02:50.896
Watch in order if you can; each episode leans on the ones before it.

32
00:02:51.276 --> 00:02:55.166
Every episode ends the same way: three things to carry away.

33
00:02:55.546 --> 00:02:58.704
If you remember only those, you have the argument.

34
00:03:00.024 --> 00:03:01.881
Three things to carry away.

35
00:03:02.921 --> 00:03:03.525
One.

36
00:03:04.565 --> 00:03:13.354
Watch in chapter order: question, baseline, inside, platform, yardstick, AI system, then what changed.

37
00:03:14.394 --> 00:03:14.951
Two.

38
00:03:15.991 --> 00:03:19.555
The spine: a claim must name the decision it changed.

39
00:03:20.595 --> 00:03:21.361
Three.

40
00:03:22.401 --> 00:03:24.445
What moved is the call to the model.

41
00:03:25.485 --> 00:03:31.278
The business boundary, the owner of the fact, and the acceptance of a change stayed put.

42
00:03:32.598 --> 00:03:35.454
Next: what this research is for, in three minutes.
